<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/intermediate/colour-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Colour theory is <strong>the physics of light meeting the psychology of perception</strong> —
    the physics lesson\'s wavelengths plus the perception lesson\'s relativity. Its core finding
    (colour is a relationship, not a property) is why palettes work and random colour samplers
    don\'t.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dimensions, mixing modes, interaction, schemes" widget="equationStep" config={colour2Cfg} />

  <h2>Working knowledge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Complements give you greys:</strong> mixing complementary colours makes the most natural shadows and neutrals — black tends to deaden colour; orange into blue\'s shadow keeps it alive.</li>
    <li><strong>Limit the palette:</strong> three colours plus white force harmonies accidental full-palettes never find — the constraint-as-creativity rule every design discipline rediscovers.</li>
    <li><strong>Warm advances, cool recedes:</strong> atmospheric perspective (the depth lesson\'s companion) — mountains get bluer and lighter with distance because air scatters blue; painters encode the physics.</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural colour is real:</strong> white = mourning in parts of Asia, red = luck in China, blue = trust in Western branding — the geography lesson\'s cultures, chromatically indexed; designers working globally check the map before shipping.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Albers, Interaction of Color (1963); Itten, The Art of Color; the physics lesson\'s light chapter.</p>
</TopicPage>
